WEBVTT

00:03.840 --> 00:04.160
All right.

00:04.160 --> 00:06.320
So now let's have a quick summary about the nulls.

00:06.360 --> 00:11.400
Nulls are special markers in SQL in order to say there is no value.

00:11.440 --> 00:12.280
It is missing.

00:12.280 --> 00:13.400
It is unknown.

00:13.400 --> 00:18.360
So nulls are not equal to zero or empty string or any blank spaces.

00:18.360 --> 00:24.200
And using nulls inside our database is going to save some storage and as well provide a strong performance

00:24.200 --> 00:25.240
in your queries.

00:25.240 --> 00:28.360
And in SQL we have different functions in order to handle the nulls.

00:28.360 --> 00:33.720
So now if you want to replace a null with the value, we can go either with the function coalesce or

00:33.760 --> 00:34.400
Isnull.

00:34.440 --> 00:39.800
Or if you want to do the opposite where you want to replace a value with null, you can go use the function

00:39.800 --> 00:40.720
null if or.

00:40.720 --> 00:44.000
In other cases, we want only to check whether there is nulls or not.

00:44.040 --> 00:50.280
We can use the is null or is not null, and we have learned as well that we have to treat the nulls,

00:50.320 --> 00:52.560
especially before doing any tasks.

00:52.560 --> 00:55.360
So that means we have to handle the nulls before doing.

00:55.360 --> 01:00.160
For example, data aggregations like average sum, max, min and so on.

01:00.160 --> 01:05.240
And we have to handle the nulls as well before doing any mathematical operations, like using the plus

01:05.280 --> 01:08.000
operator to concatenate two strings.

01:08.000 --> 01:12.840
And in some scenarios, as we learned, we have to handle the nulls as well before doing joins.

01:12.840 --> 01:16.880
And in other cases we have as well to handle the nulls before sorting the data.

01:16.880 --> 01:18.160
And we have learned as well.

01:18.200 --> 01:25.000
By combining the joins and the Isnull, we introduce new types of joins, like as we learned the left

01:25.000 --> 01:30.440
Anti-join and the right Anti-join, where we exclude the matching rows using the Isnull.

01:30.440 --> 01:36.840
And we can use the null functions in order to provide standards and data policies in our data, like

01:36.840 --> 01:40.320
using the nulls or using a default values like the unknown.

01:40.360 --> 01:41.080
All right, my friends.

01:41.080 --> 01:44.280
So with that, you have learned how to handle the nulls inside your data.

01:44.280 --> 01:47.920
And now we're going to move to a very special topic called the case statements.

01:47.920 --> 01:51.360
This is a very important tool in order to do data transformations.

01:51.360 --> 01:52.080
So let's go.
